course: dictionary
lesson: 14172
title: Parts that fit together
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A module is one separate part that fits together with other parts to make a bigger whole. Think of a LEGO brick: one brick is a module, and the whole model is built from many of them. At school, a course is often split into modules, and each module covers one topic.
explain: Use the words around the word to work it out. «The science course has five modules, one for each topic.» Five modules make up one course, and each module is one topic. So a module is one unit that forms part of something bigger.
explain: Space and computers use the same idea. «The lunar module landed two astronauts on the Moon.» Here a module is a separate part of a spacecraft. «The coder split the game into three modules, one for sound, one for scoring and one for the map.» Each module does one job.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Does «the fractions module» mean one separate unit of a bigger course? Yes, so your meaning makes sense.
fact: A module is one separate part that fits together with other parts to make a whole.
fact: «The fractions module» means one unit of a course on fractions, often with its own lessons and test.
fact: «The lunar module» was a separate part of a spacecraft; the lunar module «Eagle» landed Apollo 11 on the Moon in 1969.
fact: The International Space Station is built from modules that were joined together.
fact: In coding, programmers split a program into modules, and each module does one job.
exercise: «The science course has five modules, one for each topic.» What is a module in this sentence? | A long piece of string | A kind of computer game | The whole school building | *One separate unit of a course on one topic
exercise: «The lunar module landed on the Moon in 1969.» What kind of module is this? | A school lesson about planets | *A separate part of a spacecraft | A kind of modern house | A rule for spelling
exercise: A programmer splits a game into modules. Why does she do this? | So the game gets longer and harder to play | *So each part can do one job | So the game can run with no computer | So the game has no sound
test: «Modular homes are built from modules made in a factory.» What does modules mean in this sentence? | Rules for building a school | Big rooms with no doors | *Separate parts that fit together to make a whole | Tiny insects that live in walls
test: Why did the course have many modules? | The course had no topics at all | Each module was the same lesson repeated | *Each module covered one topic | Each module was a type of test only
exam: Ali says a module is the same thing as a whole course. Is Ali right? | No, a module is always a computer | Yes, a module is always a spacecraft | Yes, a module and a whole course are always exactly the same | *No, a module is one separate part of a bigger whole
